WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.347 --> 00:00:06.279
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:11.780
Thank you very
much, Hadley..

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:14.990
Today, I'm going to talk about
free and open-source software

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:16.500
for data science.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.250
The lens I'm going to
use to talk about it

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:23.532
is kind of the how and
why of RStudio--

00:00:23.532 --> 00:00:25.490
a little bit about how
the company got started,

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:28.670
and where our mission of
creating free and open-source

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:31.130
software derives from.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.830
But then I want to get
into the ways that tools--

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:37.520
both open-source and proprietary
for scientific and technical

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.060
computing-- are developed.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.320
How are they
financially supported?

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.280
How are they sustained?

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.650
How can you come to trust them?

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.430
And get into a little bit about
the nature of corporations

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.790
as stewards of this
sort of software--

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:56.090
and I'll ask the
question, are corporations

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:59.510
inherently sketchy as stewards
of this sort of software?

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:02.330
Spoiler-- they are.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:04.370
So then I want to talk
a little bit about what

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.270
we can do about that.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:10.700
So I'll start by a
little background

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.200
about how the
company got started

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.720
so you get a flavor for
where I was coming from,

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.070
starting the company.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:21.050
The seeds of the company go
all the way back to 1983.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:22.967
How many people here
have heard of Bill James?

00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:24.258
People have heard a Bill James?

00:01:24.258 --> 00:01:24.780
OK.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.700
So Bill James was a math
teacher in Kansas City.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:34.160
And back in 1983, I was a
avid follower of baseball,

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.180
and I absorbed all
of the analysis

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.380
about baseball from writers,
and sportscasters, and players,

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.450
and coaches, and experts.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.620
And I came to believe
a lot of things

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.840
I was hearing about
how baseball teams win

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.285
games, how you assess the value
of players and strategies.

00:01:54.285 --> 00:01:55.910
So what Bill James
did, interestingly--

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:59.570
you can see back in 1977, his
first baseball abstract was

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:01.160
this little pamphlet--

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.350
he went from that
pamphlet over 10 years

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:08.389
to a New York Times bestselling
book, where he used data--

00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:12.140
empirical data analysis
to systematically debunk

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.360
many of the assumptions
that people had

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:16.610
about how baseball works.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:18.500
And for me, as a
14-year-old, that

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.230
was really striking
that all these people

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:24.710
who had spent their lives
in the game of baseball,

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.290
who had developed conclusions
and intuitions about how it

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.480
worked, that all that
could be debunked

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.790
by systematically using data.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.903
It was shocking to me,
and that stuck with me.

00:02:36.903 --> 00:02:38.820
When I went to college,
I didn't go to college

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:39.920
for baseball studies.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.350
I went to college for
political science.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.960
And when you talk about
political science,

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.680
you get into public policy,
and public policy decisions

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.330
are made that affect
the well-being

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.950
of many, many hundreds
of millions of people.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.010
And so the same thing
that occurred to me,

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:57.260
that we've got lots
of experts and we've

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:00.290
got lots of people who've been
absorbed in different fields

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.540
and observing phenomena
for years making

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.960
these decisions, and possibly--
probably, as it turned out,

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:10.408
not actually using data
to inform those decisions.

00:03:10.408 --> 00:03:12.200
And as I've become part
of the R community,

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.225
I've realized that the
same phenomena repeats

00:03:14.225 --> 00:03:16.490
in fields like
medicine and business--

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:21.320
that people are making highly
consequential decisions,

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.260
and they're not availing
themselves of the tools

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.600
they have to really understand
how the world works.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:33.440
So this then, to
me, through college,

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.750
struck me as the fundamental
problem to solve.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:39.080
And it seemed to me that
software was an important part

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:39.750
of the answer.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.870
This is some of the
software that I use

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.760
in college and graduate school.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.000
And say what you might
about some of this software,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.750
I had the experience of
software providing leverage.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:54.770
My mind and what I could absorb
was amplified by the fact

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:58.830
that I could use the software
to better understand things.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:02.750
So from there, I went
to graduate school

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:04.820
at the University
Wisconsin Madison,

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:06.140
studying political science.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:08.278
And this is going to be
an aside that this group

00:04:08.278 --> 00:04:09.570
I think we'll find interesting.

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.528
it's not really in the
main thread of the talk,

00:04:11.528 --> 00:04:13.940
but I worked on a study
there that was studying

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:17.000
the effectiveness of
vouchers and public school

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.050
performance in Milwaukee.

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:20.810
Milwaukee was one of the
first, if not the first cities

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.090
to implement a school
voucher program,

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:25.670
and there was a study
going on in Madison

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:29.415
that was going to assess how
effective school vouchers were.

00:04:29.415 --> 00:04:30.540
And I worked on that study.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.582
So at the same time, there
was a group at Harvard

00:04:32.582 --> 00:04:36.200
that was very convinced
that the conclusions that

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:39.260
would be drawn by the folks at
Madison were going to be wrong.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:43.400
So they were very keen to
reproduce and criticize

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.090
the results of the study.

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:47.490
So they asked for the data.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.157
They said, could we
please have the data

00:04:49.157 --> 00:04:51.420
so we can do our own analysis?

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.660
So we have the data
in Paradox databases

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:57.510
that were made machine-readable,
super easy to send to them.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:59.330
But what we did
instead-- true story--

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:02.240
was we printed out all
the data and shipped

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.940
them crates of paper
so that they could

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:06.600
reenter the data on their own.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.160
So that's kind of where we were
in reproducibility and academia

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:11.330
back then.

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:12.750
So that's a true story.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:17.000
So anyway, so when
I got to Madison,

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.830
I was really excited about
data analysis and computation,

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:24.680
and I was like,
could I specialize

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:26.300
in software for data analysis?

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.940
And unfortunately, in
political science at that time,

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.590
software was definitely
not something

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:31.950
you could specialize in.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.992
And at Madison,
even data analysis

00:05:33.992 --> 00:05:36.200
was kind of barely something
you could specialize in.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:39.315
So I was kind of
in the wrong place.

00:05:39.315 --> 00:05:41.440
When I was supposed to be
doing my graduate school,

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:44.930
work I was teaching myself how
to program in C++ and teaching

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:46.730
myself how to program the Mac.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:50.210
And that inevitably led
shortly to me dropping out

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:54.677
of the program and saying, I
want to be a software engineer.

00:05:54.677 --> 00:05:56.510
I alluded to this a
little bit before-- what

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.940
fascinated me so much about
being a software engineer

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:04.340
is summed up well by this quote
from Steve Jobs, where he says,

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:06.890
what a computer is to me--
it's the most remarkable tool

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:08.100
that we've ever come up with.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:11.180
It's the equivalent of
a bicycle for our minds.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:14.780
And the other thing he cites,
when he recounted that quote,

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.700
was there was a
Scientific American study

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:21.260
that looked at the efficiency
of different modalities

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:22.790
of motion--

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:27.920
the cost of transport, calories
per gram per kilometer, as it

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:29.340
relates to body weight.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:30.800
So they looked at organisms.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.330
Salmon were super efficient.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:33.572
Horses were super efficient.

00:06:33.572 --> 00:06:35.030
They looked at
different modalities

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:37.010
of human
transportation, and they

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:42.260
found that a person on a bicycle
was by far the most efficient.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:44.210
And that's, I think,
what computers are

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:45.140
and what software is.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:47.530
So that became my
fascination, and I

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:50.520
decided I want to become
a software engineer

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:52.890
and I wanted to
build software tools.

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:56.240
So I did that for quite
a number of years.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:57.380
I built programming tools.

00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:58.370
I built research tools.

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:00.680
I built some writing tools.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.250
And the software
I worked on was--

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.000
had a couple of characteristics.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.210
One was it was
proprietary software.

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:09.530
Two was I was working
in startup companies.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:13.250
And proprietary software
worked on in startup companies

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:14.060
is almost--

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:17.210
it has the seeds
of its demise built

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:20.660
in from the beginning,
because startup companies are

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:22.070
built to be sold.

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:23.900
And usually, when
they're sold, they're,

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:27.560
in some form or fashion,
destroyed or warped.

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:29.780
And the proprietary
software is often

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:32.930
very bound up in the fortunes
and fate of the companies that

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:34.190
sponsor its development.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:36.590
So I really enjoyed
working on software tools,

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:39.260
but I found that proprietary
software in startups

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:41.690
was not something I wanted
to do anymore after that.

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:46.070
So I was searching for, what
would I like to do next?

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:49.473
And I came to the
conclusion that one

00:07:49.473 --> 00:07:51.140
of things I wanted
was I wanted to build

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:55.170
tools that were durable, that
could outlast a given company.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.170
And I wanted to build
tools that were accessible

00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:00.327
to everyone, that anyone could
use, irrespective of cost.

00:08:00.327 --> 00:08:01.910
And that led me to
the idea of working

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:02.990
on open-source software.

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:05.510
So I knew I wanted to work
on open-source software.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:09.770
I knew that I didn't want
to do software startups.

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:13.430
At that time, I found out about
R, which kind of took me back

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:16.475
to the work that I had
done in data analysis

00:08:16.475 --> 00:08:19.225
as an undergrad graduate school.

00:08:19.225 --> 00:08:20.600
I don't know how
long it took me.

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:22.100
It was definitely
less than 24 hours

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:25.082
to conclude, this is what I
want to work on, at the time,

00:08:25.082 --> 00:08:26.790
I felt like maybe for
the next 10 years--

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:28.640
now I feel like for
the rest of my career.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:32.840
So I was very lucky
to discover R.

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:36.440
And it also felt to me like
I could offer something

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.799
to the community, because I
had worked on programming tools

00:08:39.799 --> 00:08:44.360
and tools to make
people more productive

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:47.240
with complex software.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:49.970
So I also didn't
want to do a startup,

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:51.830
and I thought,
well, this is fine,

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:53.322
because I think
one or two people

00:08:53.322 --> 00:08:55.280
could actually make a
significant contribution.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:56.840
We wouldn't need
to have a startup--

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:58.590
we could just make
a contribution.

00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:00.923
And so that's when I started
working on the RStudio IDE.

00:09:00.923 --> 00:09:02.720
And I worked on it
initially by myself,

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.450
and then Joe Cheng,
who I'd worked

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:07.700
with in a couple of
previous companies,

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:10.250
joined me a few months later,
and together, we set out

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:11.600
to build the RStudio IDE.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.190
And the general
mission was to try

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:15.470
to make a contribution
to open-source software

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:18.440
for statistical computing.

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:22.690
So I want to now
say, why is free

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:25.730
and open-source software
for science and data science

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:26.620
so important?

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:30.610
At the time, I was focused
on this idea of durability

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:35.840
and accessibility, but as
I've joined the r community,

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:38.180
I've come to realize there's
lots of other good reasons

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.970
to prefer open-source
software for data science.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:43.150
So I want to get into
a little bit of that.

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:45.890
And I first want to
make a distinction

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:48.920
between different
senses of the word free.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:51.500
There's for free-- gratis--

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:54.825
and there's with little
or no restriction-- libre.

00:09:54.825 --> 00:09:56.200
And both are
relevant, obviously,

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:58.210
with open-source software.

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:01.240
Famously, Richard Stallman
summarized the difference

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:04.750
and the nature of libre as think
free as in free speech, not

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:06.310
free beer.

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:08.020
We sometimes, with
free software,

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:11.890
focused too much on the fact
that the software has no cost,

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:13.630
but the most important
thing is that it

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:16.460
comes without restrictions.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:18.790
And they actually--

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:21.460
GNU summarizes the four
essential freedoms of free

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:24.130
and open-source software, and
they mostly have to do with

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:27.620
being able to do with the
program what you wish,

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:31.300
including inspect it, modify
it, create your own derivative

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:32.630
works from it--

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:34.960
and the fact that
you are not dependent

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:37.210
upon the original purveyor
of the software to continue

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:39.560
using or evolving the software.

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:43.372
So that's actually, for science
especially, more important.

00:10:43.372 --> 00:10:44.830
The fact that it
comes without cost

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:48.790
is important-- that's
actually even more important.

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:52.020
So what are some
of the reasons why

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:54.870
we want to prefer free
and open-source software?

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:57.420
I don't need to speak
at any length about this

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:00.090
with this group, but it's
worth reflecting on the fact

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.087
that, if I use proprietary
software to do science or data

00:11:03.087 --> 00:11:04.920
science, and someone
else wants to reproduce

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:07.560
my results, at the
best, that person

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:10.440
needs to buy a license for
the software that I used.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:12.900
But at worst-- and
this often happens--

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:15.420
I can't even reproduce my
own work in the future,

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:17.880
because maybe the vendor who
created the software has gone

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:20.010
out of business, or
they've older versions

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:21.480
of their products inaccessible.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:24.960
So long-term reproducibility
is really only assured

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:28.470
by using free and
open-source software.

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:31.500
I want to point out briefly
just how important the R

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.060
community has been
in this movement

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:34.690
toward reproducibility.

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:38.370
There was an article
written in Nature in 2012

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:40.020
making this case.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:41.600
And at the time,
they cited there

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:45.120
are two systems known to enable
the packaging of code data

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:46.590
and text at the time.

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:49.800
One of those two was Sweave,
which the R community actually

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:51.720
came up with in 2002.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:54.120
So we've been at
this for 18 years--

00:11:54.120 --> 00:12:00.380
certainly longer than any other
programming language community.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:04.188
There's another consideration,
which is resiliency.

00:12:04.188 --> 00:12:06.230
As I said before, software
products and companies

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:07.430
come and go.

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:09.200
We don't want our
research, our ability

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:11.630
to reproduce the
research, tied to the fate

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:15.320
of a specific product or vendor.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:17.840
Now, a variation on this
theme is that software

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:18.770
doesn't come and go.

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:21.680
It actually stays and becomes
really, really important

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:23.900
to customers, and
then the vendor

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:26.060
decides, oh, this is
opportunity for us

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:29.030
to dramatically raise
prices and extract

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:30.920
more value from our customers.

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:35.287
So notably, the four essential
freedoms that I talked about

00:12:35.287 --> 00:12:37.370
ensure that this cannot
happen with free software.

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:40.220
There's a great example of
this from the database world,

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:41.000
where MySQL--

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:44.540
which is a GPL open-source
database-- was acquired by Sun,

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:47.570
and then subsequently, Sun
was acquired by Oracle.

00:12:47.570 --> 00:12:49.400
So Oracle, a proprietary
database vendor,

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:53.030
now owned all the
copyrights to MySQL,

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:56.930
and they were gearing up to
try to do all kinds of things

00:12:56.930 --> 00:12:59.930
to try to exploit that position.

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:02.120
Even though Oracle actually
owned all the copyrights

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:05.870
for MySQL, the community
took the code for MySQL,

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.030
forked it, and created
a another product

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:10.640
called MariaDB, and continued
on with development.

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:12.200
So only the fact
that that software

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:14.540
was free and open-source
ultimately protected

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:18.230
the community from a vendor
that was going to be abusive.

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:20.780
And we can think,
in the R community,

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:22.460
RStudio and
many other vendors

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:28.470
have provided offerings around
R. The commitments of vendors

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:29.495
can vary over time.

00:13:29.495 --> 00:13:30.620
Companies can get acquired.

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:32.480
They can shift strategies.

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:38.120
If, as a user, your primary
investment is an open-source R

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:40.940
code that will run irrespective
of any vendor's products,

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:42.410
then you're protected from that.

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:46.090
You have that resiliency.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:48.140
There's another piece,
which is participation.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:51.220
I think maybe 10
or 15 years ago,

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:54.910
many people might have believed,
well, a proprietary software

00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:58.330
vendor can enumerate and
account for all the methods

00:13:58.330 --> 00:14:01.155
that are important in a
field, and then provide that.

00:14:01.155 --> 00:14:02.530
Or a lot of people
believed that.

00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:04.990
I think now, the
explosion of innovation

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:07.360
in statistical
methodology, nobody

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:12.850
believes that-- that a single
vendor could be the filter who

00:14:12.850 --> 00:14:15.130
decides what methods are
supported, and available,

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:16.510
and easy to use.

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:20.740
So the fact that we have an
open-source ecosystem around R

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:23.050
enables what you've
seen with CRAN,

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:25.297
where there's this huge
long tail of innovation,

00:14:25.297 --> 00:14:27.130
there can be many, many
different approaches

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:29.645
to analysis, there can
be innovation in methods,

00:14:29.645 --> 00:14:31.270
and it's all supported
by the software.

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:35.020
So participation's another
fundamentally important thing.

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:38.020
And finally, what I talked
about at the beginning,

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:39.220
accessibility--

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:42.287
and data literacy is
becoming-- it's fundamentally

00:14:42.287 --> 00:14:44.620
important for organizations,
and it's also fundamentally

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:46.840
important for individuals.

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:50.080
And open-source
software allows everyone

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:52.350
to participate and use
these tools, again,

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:53.350
without regard for cost.

00:14:56.360 --> 00:15:00.710
So let's talk then a little
more generally about how

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:02.488
these tools for--

00:15:02.488 --> 00:15:05.030
not just data science, but tools
for scientific and technical

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:07.832
computing get built--
both proprietary tools

00:15:07.832 --> 00:15:09.290
and open-source
tools-- how they're

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:13.070
built, how they're funded,
what the underlying structural

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:15.830
incentives are.

00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:18.980
Looking at the history of
scientific and technical

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:21.740
computing companies,
these are leaders.

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:24.200
If you don't recognize
them by company name,

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:25.970
you certainly recognize
their products--

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:28.330
SAS, MATLAB, Mathematica.

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:31.400
And they actually have
some significant shared

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:34.580
characteristics-- all of them
started in academia by one

00:15:34.580 --> 00:15:37.630
or two people, and over the
first few years of the project,

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:39.390
it was only one or two people.

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:41.660
And then eventually,
they organically spread

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:44.750
within academia, and then
grew into becoming companies.

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:47.480
All of these
companies, I would say,

00:15:47.480 --> 00:15:51.110
if you look at their
about page or read

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:55.070
the writings of their founders,
or talks by their founders,

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:56.660
they identify their
principal mission

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:59.510
is to support research
and science, not profit.

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:02.070
And consequently, these
are all private companies.

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:04.100
None of these are
publicly traded.

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:06.470
They're all closely held
by the original group

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:07.940
that founded them.

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:12.260
And of course, notably, they all
make proprietary software, not

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:13.400
open-source software.

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:17.490
So what's problematic
about this mirrors

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:21.570
what I said about what's good
about open-source software.

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:23.130
We have reproducibility
problems,

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:26.250
accessibility
problems, centralizing

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:29.880
decisions about what
methods are supported.

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:32.910
And there's also that
risk of companies

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:36.150
that want to perpetuate
themselves being incentivized

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:37.620
to hold their customers hostage.

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:40.447
So those are some problems.

00:16:40.447 --> 00:16:42.280
One really good thing
about these companies,

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:43.697
though, is that
they actually have

00:16:43.697 --> 00:16:46.270
an economic engine that they
can use to fund development.

00:16:46.270 --> 00:16:48.330
And without some kind
of an economic engine,

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:50.460
oftentimes, insufficient
progress isn't made.

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:52.050
The tools actually
don't reach the bar

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:54.810
where people can use them
to solve all the problems.

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:56.490
So they have some
significant drawbacks,

00:16:56.490 --> 00:17:00.150
but they have that benefit of
having this economic engine.

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:02.454
So now, let's consider
open-source tools

00:17:02.454 --> 00:17:04.079
for scientific and
technical computing.

00:17:04.079 --> 00:17:06.300
The ones that the
people here are probably

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:10.680
most hands-on familiar with are
the ones the R and Python data

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:11.760
science ecosystems.

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:13.890
But there have
been lots of tools

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:17.520
before that-- notably,
SageMath and GNU Octave.

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:19.380
And these all have
roots quite similar

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:20.910
to the proprietary
software vendors.

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:22.920
They started in academia.

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:25.680
They grew very slowly and
organically at the beginning.

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:28.290
And the founders
of these projects

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:31.050
also were concerned
principally with supporting

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:34.950
research and science, and they
wanted to protect the software.

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:37.950
Their means of protecting it
was to make it open-source,

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:40.913
because at the time a lot
of these projects emerged,

00:17:40.913 --> 00:17:42.330
the idea of
open-source had gained

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:46.680
more currency-- the viability of
open-source had more currency.

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:49.450
Linux had achieved some success.

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:54.080
So this is the approach that
these project have taken.

00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:57.950
And so what's problematic here
is, do these projects have

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:01.850
enough funding to
sustain momentum and get

00:18:01.850 --> 00:18:04.100
where users want them to be?

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:07.190
And honestly, over half
of software engineering

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:10.730
is solving boring
problems, and sometimes

00:18:10.730 --> 00:18:14.350
you just need to
have the size of team

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:16.100
and a commitment of a
team to go and solve

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:17.600
those difficult and
boring problems.

00:18:20.312 --> 00:18:22.520
Getting enough resources,
as I say, in these projects

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:23.820
can be a challenge.

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:27.020
And there's also an issue
of project organization.

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:28.820
Are these projects
cohesive enough

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:30.710
to deliver the software
that users need?

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:35.168
And then significantly,
are organizations

00:18:35.168 --> 00:18:36.710
who are making
decisions about taking

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:38.752
big long-term dependencies
on software projects--

00:18:38.752 --> 00:18:41.690
are they comfortable adopting
the software without visibility

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:43.400
into the long-term
health of the project?

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:46.820
Will the project be around?

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:49.250
So there's different ways
that people have come up with

00:18:49.250 --> 00:18:51.680
to find open-source development.

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:52.340
There's grants.

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:55.047
And Jupyter has actually
done quite a bit with grants.

00:18:55.047 --> 00:18:57.380
There's probably a natural
limit to how much funding you

00:18:57.380 --> 00:18:59.690
can get from grants, but
Jupyter's done quite well

00:18:59.690 --> 00:19:01.965
with that.

00:19:01.965 --> 00:19:04.340
Open-source software can be
funded by companies that have

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:07.007
an interest in the software, and
Linux is the best success story

00:19:07.007 --> 00:19:10.070
here, where lots of really large
companies have had an interest

00:19:10.070 --> 00:19:11.330
in having a free--

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:15.440
robust, free Unix operating
system all contributed to it.

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:17.987
And that's the initial
model for Ursa Labs working

00:19:17.987 --> 00:19:20.570
on Apache Arrow, where there's
a bunch of companies, including

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:23.810
RStudio, that invested
in building the software,

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:26.520
because we think it's
going to benefit our users.

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:29.460
There's also the traditional
method of venture capital,

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:33.200
but if you look at those
companies I talked about--

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:37.157
MathWorks, SAS, Wolfram--
they've been around.

00:19:37.157 --> 00:19:39.740
The evolution of their products
and the adoption that products

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:42.440
took like 30 or 40 years.

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:44.690
They've been run
for a long time.

00:19:44.690 --> 00:19:47.270
The venture capital model
is much, much shorter,

00:19:47.270 --> 00:19:50.300
so I don't think it fits
particularly well with building

00:19:50.300 --> 00:19:52.385
open-source tools for science.

00:19:52.385 --> 00:19:54.260
So the question is, are
any of these-- do any

00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:57.050
these models actually work?

00:19:57.050 --> 00:19:59.180
And this question
was asked rather--

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:03.650
or answered rather
pointedly by a gentleman

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:06.410
from Wolfram, because
apparently, Wolfram gets

00:20:06.410 --> 00:20:09.680
asked all the time, why isn't
Mathematica open-source?

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:13.160
And so he wrote a
blog post, 12 reasons

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:14.720
why it's not open-source.

00:20:14.720 --> 00:20:18.445
And I'm not going to read
these reasons out loud to you.

00:20:18.445 --> 00:20:19.820
If you take a
quick scan of them,

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:23.540
I think you get a sense
for what the reasons are.

00:20:23.540 --> 00:20:28.520
And I would say, generally,
I agree with this analysis,

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:33.620
to the extent that I do believe
that you have to assemble

00:20:33.620 --> 00:20:35.870
a group that works together
to achieve a set of shared

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:37.820
goals over a sustained
period of time,

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:39.800
you have to have strong
technical leadership

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:43.220
to solve hard problems, and
you do need a financial engine

00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:44.987
that can compensate
talented people

00:20:44.987 --> 00:20:46.070
to work on these problems.

00:20:46.070 --> 00:20:50.240
That's kind of the
main gist of his case.

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:51.980
I agree that those
things are required,

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:53.960
but I actually think
it's possible to do it

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:55.460
with open-source software.

00:20:55.460 --> 00:21:00.260
And I think that the history of
RStudio demonstrates that.

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:04.640
So if we look a little bit at
the history of the company,

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:05.840
2008--

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:08.390
late 2008 is, I believe,
when we started.

00:21:08.390 --> 00:21:10.310
It was, as I said
before, just one,

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:14.040
or two, or three people working
on open-source software.

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:18.920
And then about seven years ago,
we decided that we weren't--

00:21:18.920 --> 00:21:21.847
we thought we could
do a lot more with--

00:21:21.847 --> 00:21:24.180
just having three people
working on open-source software

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:27.630
was fine, but we thought there
was potential to do a lot more.

00:21:27.630 --> 00:21:29.240
And so we made a
decision at that time

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:32.240
to build a company around
the open-source work

00:21:32.240 --> 00:21:33.980
we had done, with
the notion that we

00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:36.590
could fund lots more
open-source development,

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:38.900
if we had a company
producing revenue

00:21:38.900 --> 00:21:39.900
to fund the development.

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.860
So we did that, and you can
see, over the last seven years

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:46.100
or so, we've produced a huge
amount of open-source software.

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:48.350
We have, I think, over
250 open-source projects

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:51.590
that we are active
developers on,

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:53.630
and we have this set
of commercial products

00:21:53.630 --> 00:21:54.710
that we sell.

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:57.680
And we've grown from that
original one, or two, or three,

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:00.560
six people to now
over 150 people.

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:04.220
Our company is
profitable, so we're

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:07.130
able to take the revenue
from our commercial products

00:22:07.130 --> 00:22:08.240
and fund it back into--

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:09.972
feed back into
open-source development,

00:22:09.972 --> 00:22:11.930
and then continue, as
the company grows to grow

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:13.410
that commitment over time.

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:17.600
So I think we've figured out
a model by which we can still

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:19.250
produce open-source
software, but do it

00:22:19.250 --> 00:22:23.602
in this sustainable,
well-funded way.

00:22:23.602 --> 00:22:25.560
We sometimes refer to
this as a virtuous cycle,

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:28.710
where we create open-source
software, and lots and lots

00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:30.510
of people-- because
it's accessible,

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:32.580
lots and lots people use it.

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:35.760
And when lots and lots of people
use software, what happens

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:38.850
is large organizations
start to adopt the software.

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:40.320
And large
organizations typically

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:43.590
have deployment, and management,
and scalability requirements

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:46.140
that are different than
individuals or small groups,

00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:47.723
and that creates an
opportunity for us

00:22:47.723 --> 00:22:50.700
to build products to solve those
problems, which then gives us

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:53.640
revenue to invest back
in open-source tools.

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:55.500
Now, you'll notice
here-- it's pretty

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:58.950
subtle on this slide-- there's
a line between these things.

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:01.530
And you might ask the question,
well, where's that line,

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:04.320
and what's where's the
assurance that a company like

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:06.030
RStudio--

00:23:06.030 --> 00:23:08.250
as I said, corporations
are inherently sketchy--

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:10.380
isn't going to move
that line at some point?

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:13.110
Let me give you the
operative principle that--

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:14.480
for us, that's behind that line.

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:16.980
The operative principle has to
do with preserving those four

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:18.030
freedoms.

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:22.170
So the core libraries, packages,
protocols, file formats,

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:25.440
even productivity tools,
like RStudio IDE need to be

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:29.790
open-source so that users who
adopt the software experience

00:23:29.790 --> 00:23:32.070
the benefits of those four
freedoms, and essentially,

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:33.440
are not--

00:23:33.440 --> 00:23:36.570
their work is not locked in
to the products of a given

00:23:36.570 --> 00:23:38.070
software vendor.

00:23:38.070 --> 00:23:41.100
The tools that we create
to facilitate adoption of R

00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:44.940
in large, complex environments,
those tools are commercial.

00:23:44.940 --> 00:23:47.790
Those are tools that
candidly, if we're not

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:50.280
providing good
value to customers,

00:23:50.280 --> 00:23:53.670
they can continue using R,
continue using the Tidyverse,

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:56.620
R Markdown, Shiny-- everything
without those tools.

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:59.700
So for us, we have to
continue to offer a good value

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:01.780
proposition or customers
could walk away.

00:24:01.780 --> 00:24:05.040
That's because the core software
preserves the four freedoms.

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:07.410
So that's where
we draw the line,

00:24:07.410 --> 00:24:10.740
and how we think about
open-source versus commercial.

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:13.230
So I think we've
managed to create

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:17.320
a new kind of scientific and
technical computing company.

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:22.770
I think we have those attributes
that the gentleman from Wolfram

00:24:22.770 --> 00:24:24.960
cited, which is that we
have a financial engine

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:28.800
that we can work on this in a
sustained way for many years

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:31.980
and apply adequate resources.

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:33.947
We also, like those
companies, think

00:24:33.947 --> 00:24:36.030
it's critical that we
remain independent to pursue

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:37.590
our mission.

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:40.860
But we've managed
to do this in a way

00:24:40.860 --> 00:24:43.110
where the core software
is open-source,

00:24:43.110 --> 00:24:46.050
and we don't have lock-in
the way the software--

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:48.300
and that is by design.

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:53.280
So we're pleased with this, but
it begs the question, again,

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:56.100
I said about corporations.

00:24:56.100 --> 00:24:58.180
Are we trustworthy?

00:24:58.180 --> 00:25:00.150
And I would say we're
not, at face value,

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:03.750
trustworthy, because in
today's world, corporations--

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:06.000
And I'm going to get into
this in a little more detail

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:08.890
in a minute-- they are
pure profit maximizers.

00:25:08.890 --> 00:25:12.630
And so they're-- by default, you
shouldn't trust corporations.

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:16.260
I think the R community
needs more than,

00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:21.330
we're a corporation that
has acted well in the past,

00:25:21.330 --> 00:25:24.640
in order to place their trust
in the work that we're doing.

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:27.000
And I think customers
need to trust

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:30.060
that we're looking for a
relationship of mutual benefit,

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:31.890
and that we're not
going to exploit

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:34.800
our position as a software
vendor in the future,

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:36.780
as has happened with
other proprietary software

00:25:36.780 --> 00:25:37.770
companies in the past.

00:25:40.460 --> 00:25:43.110
We need to make our motivation
as clear and transparent

00:25:43.110 --> 00:25:45.180
as possible, and we
need to try to build

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:46.710
more real, long-term trust.

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:48.270
And I think some
of that has to do

00:25:48.270 --> 00:25:50.640
with the nature
of corporations--

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:53.550
how corporations work, and how
maybe corporations actually

00:25:53.550 --> 00:25:55.380
need to evolve.

00:25:55.380 --> 00:25:56.610
So I'm going to go back.

00:25:56.610 --> 00:26:00.000
This is a little bit elemental,
but I think it's not something

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.580
that I had thought really
carefully and critically

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:05.400
about until a few years ago.

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:06.780
What actually is a corporation?

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:09.190
Why do they even exist?

00:26:09.190 --> 00:26:11.700
And what a corporation
is is actually--

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:13.920
it's a virtual person.

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:18.060
So it's acting as a
person-- legally a person--

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:19.950
but it's actually--
it's constituted

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:21.580
by a group of people.

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:24.530
So why do we even
have corporations?

00:26:24.530 --> 00:26:27.350
Well, what did we do
before corporations?

00:26:27.350 --> 00:26:29.780
Before corporations, any
business that existed

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:33.830
was either undertaken by
an individual or maybe

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:35.610
a partnership of individuals.

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:38.210
And so the individuals were
actually personally liable

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:39.890
for everything the
enterprise did,

00:26:39.890 --> 00:26:41.983
and when a contract was
made between a business

00:26:41.983 --> 00:26:43.400
and another party,
it was actually

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:46.790
a contract with the individual
or the group of individuals.

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:49.040
So when someone left a
company, the contracts actually

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:50.390
had to be renegotiated.

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:53.707
Business was really
fundamentally person to person,

00:26:53.707 --> 00:26:55.790
and that meant that anything
that a business could

00:26:55.790 --> 00:26:59.840
accomplish was bounded by
what one person's assets could

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:01.940
accomplish and
what liability one

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:06.390
person was willing to accrue.

00:27:06.390 --> 00:27:08.270
So as the industrial
age progressed,

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:12.290
this model didn't seem adequate,
and the first corporations

00:27:12.290 --> 00:27:15.200
were actually formed by royal
act, the East India Company

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:18.320
being one of the most
significant examples of that.

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:21.830
And then, as governments
realized that we needed things

00:27:21.830 --> 00:27:25.490
like bridges, railways,
banks, utilities,

00:27:25.490 --> 00:27:27.263
we need to operate
on a different scale

00:27:27.263 --> 00:27:29.180
that individuals have
been able to operate at,

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:33.020
so they created this instrument
called a corporation that they

00:27:33.020 --> 00:27:37.520
believed needed the sort
of absolving of liability

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:41.930
and additional capital that the
corporate model provided for.

00:27:41.930 --> 00:27:44.510
So if you think about
then the essential nature

00:27:44.510 --> 00:27:47.030
of a corporation,
it's an institution

00:27:47.030 --> 00:27:50.450
that's actually
created by government

00:27:50.450 --> 00:27:52.130
or to benefit the
societies they govern.

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:55.430
That was the original
purpose of corporations.

00:27:55.430 --> 00:27:58.430
And this worked very well, and
served to the public benefit

00:27:58.430 --> 00:27:59.630
in many in many regards.

00:27:59.630 --> 00:28:02.960
But if you look at
that second bullet,

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:05.600
about without fear of
personal liability,

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:08.180
that actually is a
recipe for bad behavior,

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:10.290
and we've seen that
play out as well.

00:28:10.290 --> 00:28:14.120
So what we have today with
corporations has worked well,

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:16.360
but I think we're seeing
it crack a little bit

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:17.880
in the contemporary world.

00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:20.720
So if you think about the
legal theories of what

00:28:20.720 --> 00:28:23.500
the primary purpose
of a corporation is,

00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:25.760
there's actually two
competing theories.

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:27.433
One is stakeholder.

00:28:27.433 --> 00:28:28.850
This is kind of
the original idea,

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:32.300
and the original idea that
I've been advocating for,

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:36.320
which is that the corporation
is created by the government,

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:38.180
and therefore, has
a social function.

00:28:38.180 --> 00:28:40.730
And the directors and officers
of that corporation should

00:28:40.730 --> 00:28:44.000
consider all the stakeholders
affected by the corporation--

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:47.425
employees, the environment,
the community, shareholders--

00:28:47.425 --> 00:28:48.800
looking at
everybody's interests,

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:50.850
when they make decisions.

00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:53.600
There's another theory,
which is shareholder primacy,

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:57.860
and it's a pretty
raw notion, which

00:28:57.860 --> 00:28:59.690
is the purpose of
a corporation is

00:28:59.690 --> 00:29:01.430
to maximize value
for shareholders

00:29:01.430 --> 00:29:02.990
within the bounds of law--

00:29:02.990 --> 00:29:04.370
really narrow.

00:29:04.370 --> 00:29:08.330
And sadly, that is
actually the legal theory

00:29:08.330 --> 00:29:12.950
that has won in
Anglo-American legal systems.

00:29:12.950 --> 00:29:19.120
Shareholder primacy is the law
that we live under currently.

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:21.370
There's been a couple
significant cases

00:29:21.370 --> 00:29:23.980
that establish that-- or many
cases that have built that up.

00:29:23.980 --> 00:29:25.780
A couple of the more
significant ones

00:29:25.780 --> 00:29:29.860
was, in 1919, Ford
decided that they wanted

00:29:29.860 --> 00:29:33.307
to produce less
expensive products

00:29:33.307 --> 00:29:35.890
and pay their employees more,
so they stopped paying dividends

00:29:35.890 --> 00:29:37.400
to shareholders--

00:29:37.400 --> 00:29:40.007
again, balancing the needs of
all different constituencies.

00:29:40.007 --> 00:29:42.340
And the Michigan Supreme Court
said, that's not a thing.

00:29:42.340 --> 00:29:43.590
You're not allowed to do that.

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:45.520
Your purpose is to
create profit first

00:29:45.520 --> 00:29:47.590
for shareholders, full stop.

00:29:47.590 --> 00:29:49.720
You can't do that.

00:29:49.720 --> 00:29:52.870
Another case-- a little
more contemporary--

00:29:52.870 --> 00:29:56.290
Revlon was faced with
an acquisition proposal

00:29:56.290 --> 00:29:57.273
that actually--

00:29:57.273 --> 00:29:58.690
that appealed to
the shareholders.

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:01.297
It was a good deal for
them, but the board

00:30:01.297 --> 00:30:03.880
felt that it was going to not
be a good deal for the employees

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:07.098
or the bondholders-- people
who actually held debt.

00:30:07.098 --> 00:30:09.640
And so they tried to block the
acquisition, and in this case,

00:30:09.640 --> 00:30:12.400
the Delaware Supreme
Court rejected the idea

00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:14.860
that they had the duty
or even the option

00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:17.540
to consider the
interests of stakeholders

00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:18.980
other than shareholders.

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:20.813
So there's been a number
of cases like that,

00:30:20.813 --> 00:30:23.770
and that's kind of where we
stand with corporate law.

00:30:23.770 --> 00:30:25.630
And many people
find this lacking.

00:30:29.102 --> 00:30:30.810
Companies, while
they're pursuing profit,

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:33.960
can create lots and
lots of public health

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:35.640
and environmental problems.

00:30:35.640 --> 00:30:37.800
They can create systemic
risks, as we saw

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:40.583
with recent financial crises.

00:30:40.583 --> 00:30:42.000
It leads back to
the question-- is

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:43.680
that we've given
these corporations

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:45.570
a special legal status.

00:30:45.570 --> 00:30:49.290
Does that carry any reciprocal
obligation to the public good?

00:30:49.290 --> 00:30:52.320
And it's, shouldn't
companies be able to consider

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:54.970
the welfare of their own
employees and their community,

00:30:54.970 --> 00:30:56.670
when they make decisions?

00:30:56.670 --> 00:30:59.730
A lot of the bad behavior
you see by corporations

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:02.790
is precisely because they
can't consider-- legally

00:31:02.790 --> 00:31:05.830
can't consider these things.

00:31:05.830 --> 00:31:08.700
So in response to
this, a bunch of states

00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:11.720
have actually adopted
legislation that permit--

00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:13.200
note, permit, not require--

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.660
permit directors
to consider things

00:31:15.660 --> 00:31:18.970
other than shareholder value.

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:21.790
And now we've seen recently,
this last year, there's

00:31:21.790 --> 00:31:23.790
this group called the
Business Roundtable that's

00:31:23.790 --> 00:31:28.120
composed of a bunch of CEOs of
mostly big public companies.

00:31:28.120 --> 00:31:30.538
And for the first
time since 1997,

00:31:30.538 --> 00:31:32.580
they in a public statement
said that corporations

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:36.090
shouldn't exist solely
to serve shareholders.

00:31:36.090 --> 00:31:39.900
I think people are sensing
that this regime is wrong.

00:31:39.900 --> 00:31:41.790
This isn't really talking
about fundamentally

00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:43.000
changing the system.

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:45.600
This is just more saying,
we have dissatisfaction

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:46.430
with the system.

00:31:46.430 --> 00:31:49.560
So what can be done
to change this system?

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:53.030
So I don't know how many of you
have heard of the company AND1.

00:31:53.030 --> 00:31:54.460
Have people have heard of AND1?

00:31:54.460 --> 00:31:56.360
It's a basketball shoe company.

00:31:56.360 --> 00:31:58.040
And I think, as
it will turn out,

00:31:58.040 --> 00:32:00.680
this company will
be very significant

00:32:00.680 --> 00:32:05.040
in the history of corporate
law, and perhaps, of capitalism.

00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:08.130
So let me tell you a
little bit about AND1--

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:10.620
baseball shoe company
founded in 1993.

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:14.220
They were a socially
responsible business.

00:32:14.220 --> 00:32:17.580
They treated employees really
well, had great benefits.

00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:21.720
They allocated 5% of their
profits to local charities.

00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:24.950
And significantly,
they're a shoe company,

00:32:24.950 --> 00:32:26.490
so they had overseas factories.

00:32:26.490 --> 00:32:28.882
They worked to implement
a supplier code of conduct

00:32:28.882 --> 00:32:30.840
to make sure that workers
in overseas factories

00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:35.820
were treated well, safely,
had good wages, et cetera.

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:38.490
So they were a socially
responsible business

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:42.360
before that became something
more widely practiced.

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:44.325
So what happened to AND1?

00:32:44.325 --> 00:32:46.200
Well, they were pretty
small in the mid '90s.

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:49.620
They took on external
investors in 1999,

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:53.790
and it turned out that they
grew quite a bit over that span,

00:32:53.790 --> 00:32:55.920
from '95 to 2001.

00:32:55.920 --> 00:32:57.930
But then, ultimately,
they had competition

00:32:57.930 --> 00:33:00.930
from Nike and others,
and their sales dropped,

00:33:00.930 --> 00:33:03.225
and they were forced
to sell the company.

00:33:03.225 --> 00:33:05.100
And what was really
surprising and disturbing

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:06.510
to the founders was--

00:33:06.510 --> 00:33:08.662
who created this
company with the idea--

00:33:08.662 --> 00:33:10.620
we created the company,
we control the company,

00:33:10.620 --> 00:33:12.720
we want to build a socially
responsible business.

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:15.000
When they went to
sell the company,

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:18.970
it was done exclusively to
maximize shareholder value.

00:33:18.970 --> 00:33:20.100
And so after the sale--

00:33:20.100 --> 00:33:23.100
and they could do
nothing about this--

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:24.775
all those commitments
to employees

00:33:24.775 --> 00:33:26.400
or overseas workers
and local community

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:27.630
were just stripped away.

00:33:27.630 --> 00:33:30.180
So they were shocked
and disappointed,

00:33:30.180 --> 00:33:33.640
and it made them think
something needs to change.

00:33:33.640 --> 00:33:37.380
So they got together
with a friend of theirs

00:33:37.380 --> 00:33:41.730
to start a nonprofit called B
Lab, and the idea behind B Lab

00:33:41.730 --> 00:33:44.530
was to create a new form
of corporate governance.

00:33:44.530 --> 00:33:46.530
So they created the
nonprofit, and they actually

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:50.430
created a new corporate
structure called a benefit

00:33:50.430 --> 00:33:52.050
corporation.

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:55.650
And this is a reaction to the
shareholder primacy regime,

00:33:55.650 --> 00:33:57.510
where the directors
of the company

00:33:57.510 --> 00:34:01.620
are legally required to account
for all of the stakeholders--

00:34:01.620 --> 00:34:04.610
community, employees--
in their decisions.

00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:06.360
It's a legal requirement,
not an option.

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:08.565
And they also name a
public beneficial purpose

00:34:08.565 --> 00:34:10.440
as part of their charter
that, again, they're

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:11.969
accountable to pursue--

00:34:11.969 --> 00:34:15.000
legally accountable to pursue.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:17.340
I won't read this
in detail, but you

00:34:17.340 --> 00:34:19.530
can see a little bit of
the actual legislation

00:34:19.530 --> 00:34:22.020
behind Delaware public
benefit corporations,

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:25.199
and it puts a stringent legal
requirement on the directors

00:34:25.199 --> 00:34:27.000
to act in a different
way, and to not use

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:29.770
the shareholder privacy regime.

00:34:29.770 --> 00:34:33.060
So this nonprofit
has actually been

00:34:33.060 --> 00:34:37.260
successful in getting 34
states to pass legislation that

00:34:37.260 --> 00:34:39.179
permit benefit corporations.

00:34:39.179 --> 00:34:40.730
There's currently
over 7,000 of them.

00:34:40.730 --> 00:34:41.730
These are some examples.

00:34:41.730 --> 00:34:43.842
You've probably heard of
some of these companies,

00:34:43.842 --> 00:34:46.050
and you might not have known
that they were a benefit

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:47.820
corporation.

00:34:47.820 --> 00:34:51.980
In addition to these companies,
there are some public company--

00:34:51.980 --> 00:34:54.100
so the line at the top
are public companies.

00:34:54.100 --> 00:34:55.475
They're not benefit
corporations,

00:34:55.475 --> 00:34:57.870
but they have
wholly-owned subsidiaries

00:34:57.870 --> 00:34:59.670
that are, in fact,
benefit corporations.

00:34:59.670 --> 00:35:03.480
So I think this benefit
corporation idea

00:35:03.480 --> 00:35:06.210
has the seeds of what it takes
to transform what corporations

00:35:06.210 --> 00:35:08.270
are and how they
relate to their world,

00:35:08.270 --> 00:35:10.980
and to their communities, and
all of their stakeholders.

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:15.820
And it kind of takes this idea
of good companies that has

00:35:15.820 --> 00:35:18.660
existed over the last 20 years--
whether that be about Energy

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:22.050
Star compliance, or fair
trade, or organic food--

00:35:22.050 --> 00:35:26.300
and it elevates that to
the idea of good companies.

00:35:26.300 --> 00:35:28.050
So most of you or many
of you can probably

00:35:28.050 --> 00:35:30.330
guess what the next slide
of this presentation's

00:35:30.330 --> 00:35:31.710
going to be.

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:33.480
I'm really happy
to announce today

00:35:33.480 --> 00:35:37.335
that we are now a certified
Delaware Benefit Corporation.

00:35:37.335 --> 00:35:39.382
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:52.300 --> 00:35:56.020
So we actually have a new name.

00:35:56.020 --> 00:35:59.730
We're no longer RStudio,
Inc. We're RStudio, PBC.

00:35:59.730 --> 00:36:02.610
And we've always tried to run
this-- the company this way.

00:36:02.610 --> 00:36:04.485
That's always been
what we've tried to do,

00:36:04.485 --> 00:36:06.360
but now it's actually
baked into our charter.

00:36:06.360 --> 00:36:07.652
It's part of our corporate DNA.

00:36:07.652 --> 00:36:09.960
It's a requirement,
not something

00:36:09.960 --> 00:36:13.520
that we do at our discretion.

00:36:13.520 --> 00:36:15.633
As part of it, we actually
name a public benefit,

00:36:15.633 --> 00:36:17.300
and that actually
goes into our charter.

00:36:17.300 --> 00:36:19.700
This is our public benefit.

00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:22.342
And you note that
we cite the creation

00:36:22.342 --> 00:36:24.050
of free and open-source
software for data

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:26.467
science, scientific research,
and technical communication.

00:36:26.467 --> 00:36:28.490
It's a little bit broader
than data science.

00:36:28.490 --> 00:36:30.350
Today, we were
just data science,

00:36:30.350 --> 00:36:34.467
but I'm optimistic that we have
created a model that actually

00:36:34.467 --> 00:36:36.800
is a better model for scientific
and technical software.

00:36:36.800 --> 00:36:38.967
So someday, I think it
would be nice to do more

00:36:38.967 --> 00:36:40.550
within scientific
computing, so that's

00:36:40.550 --> 00:36:46.310
why we wrote the public benefit
a little bit more broadly.

00:36:46.310 --> 00:36:48.682
As part of being a public
benefit corporation,

00:36:48.682 --> 00:36:50.390
we actually will
release an annual report

00:36:50.390 --> 00:36:54.800
describing how we've served
our public beneficial purpose.

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:58.760
We've posted the first of
those reports on our website.

00:36:58.760 --> 00:37:01.370
A few highlights-- we
provide some metrics

00:37:01.370 --> 00:37:04.010
around our investment
in open-source projects.

00:37:04.010 --> 00:37:07.040
As I said before,
there's over 250 of them.

00:37:07.040 --> 00:37:09.830
We dedicate over half of the
company's engineering resources

00:37:09.830 --> 00:37:11.060
to open-source.

00:37:11.060 --> 00:37:12.793
Right now, we have 36
full-time engineers

00:37:12.793 --> 00:37:14.210
that work on
open-source software,

00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:16.743
and that's broken down
by project in the report.

00:37:16.743 --> 00:37:18.410
And there have been
hundreds of millions

00:37:18.410 --> 00:37:21.303
of downloads of our open-source
products and packages.

00:37:21.303 --> 00:37:22.970
So these are metrics
we're going to keep

00:37:22.970 --> 00:37:25.630
reporting on every year.

00:37:25.630 --> 00:37:28.250
So in addition to being
a benefit corporation,

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:31.598
the B Lab is actually created
a certification program

00:37:31.598 --> 00:37:33.890
that both looks at whether
you've changed your charter,

00:37:33.890 --> 00:37:37.650
but also looks at your impact
specifically on your workers,

00:37:37.650 --> 00:37:39.260
customers, community,
and environment,

00:37:39.260 --> 00:37:41.890
and actually rates you
across a bunch of categories.

00:37:41.890 --> 00:37:44.960
And so we're happy to also
share that we've been certified

00:37:44.960 --> 00:37:47.240
as a B Corp by the B Lab.

00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:49.340
a You can see our
impact report there,

00:37:49.340 --> 00:37:53.960
which actually has all those
ratings, is also available now.

00:37:53.960 --> 00:37:57.500
So some of this, though,
begs the question--

00:37:57.500 --> 00:38:00.920
we've become a B Corp, and that
reflects, I think, who we are,

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:03.500
and what we're about,
and what we aim to be.

00:38:03.500 --> 00:38:08.250
But it also doesn't necessarily
talk about the future.

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:11.000
And I want to tell everyone
here that our plan is

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:13.190
to remain an independent
company, to never sell

00:38:13.190 --> 00:38:13.700
the company.

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:17.210
Yes.

00:38:17.210 --> 00:38:18.670
So that is what
we're going to do.

00:38:18.670 --> 00:38:20.670
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:21.700 --> 00:38:23.730
But how can we actually
make that happen?

00:38:23.730 --> 00:38:25.970
How can we provide assurance
that's going to happen?

00:38:25.970 --> 00:38:28.970
We do have outside investors
as minority shareholders.

00:38:28.970 --> 00:38:31.250
And prior to this
conversion, we actually

00:38:31.250 --> 00:38:33.200
had written into the
financing documents,

00:38:33.200 --> 00:38:37.250
I had special rights that
I could block, what I say,

00:38:37.250 --> 00:38:38.240
undesirable outcomes.

00:38:38.240 --> 00:38:41.250
I could individually block
the sale of the company.

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:46.490
But just saying that I can block
that the sale of the company

00:38:46.490 --> 00:38:49.910
isn't really fully reassuring,
because what if I die?

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:51.590
What if I change my mind?

00:38:51.590 --> 00:38:54.027
So it's a little overreliant
on one individual,

00:38:54.027 --> 00:38:55.610
so we also, along
with the transition,

00:38:55.610 --> 00:38:59.030
made some changes to how
those shares are held

00:38:59.030 --> 00:39:00.690
and how the rights
are exercised.

00:39:00.690 --> 00:39:02.720
So now, there's actually
a group of people

00:39:02.720 --> 00:39:05.580
all inside the company
who exercise those rights.

00:39:05.580 --> 00:39:10.380
So if I die, or change my
mind, or anything like that,

00:39:10.380 --> 00:39:14.290
we still have those
protections in place.

00:39:14.290 --> 00:39:16.880
And before I conclude,
I wanted to also talk

00:39:16.880 --> 00:39:23.638
about who ultimately benefits
from RStudio's success.

00:39:23.638 --> 00:39:25.180
As I've said in the
talk, we've tried

00:39:25.180 --> 00:39:27.190
to build a company
where we have lots

00:39:27.190 --> 00:39:30.820
of beneficiaries-- all of our
stakeholders are beneficiaries.

00:39:30.820 --> 00:39:33.520
We do have shareholders,
and the traditional way

00:39:33.520 --> 00:39:36.520
that shareholders
get remunerated it

00:39:36.520 --> 00:39:39.000
usually either selling
the company going public.

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:40.345
That is not our plan.

00:39:40.345 --> 00:39:42.220
So what we're going to
do is take our profits

00:39:42.220 --> 00:39:43.780
and use those to
purchase stock back

00:39:43.780 --> 00:39:45.560
from our shareholders over time.

00:39:45.560 --> 00:39:48.520
So once we've met
that commitment,

00:39:48.520 --> 00:39:50.890
we are going to dedicate
a substantial portion

00:39:50.890 --> 00:39:55.690
of our profits to
philanthropic causes that

00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:57.610
relate to our mission
of open-source software

00:39:57.610 --> 00:39:58.780
and open science.

00:39:58.780 --> 00:40:01.690
And those donations--
we've documented

00:40:01.690 --> 00:40:04.400
in this year's annual report
the donations that we've made.

00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:07.390
But as we are able to
purchase stock back

00:40:07.390 --> 00:40:09.640
from our shareholders and
dedicate more of our profits

00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:11.980
to these donations, we'll
also report specifically

00:40:11.980 --> 00:40:14.770
on that in our annual
public benefit report.

00:40:14.770 --> 00:40:17.200
So thank you all
very much for helping

00:40:17.200 --> 00:40:20.630
us to build this company
and build this community.

00:40:20.630 --> 00:40:23.140
It's been an incredible
experience, far

00:40:23.140 --> 00:40:25.330
exceeding anything I
could have ever hoped for,

00:40:25.330 --> 00:40:27.820
and I'm excited for
what the future holds.

00:40:27.820 --> 00:40:28.630
Thank you.

00:40:28.630 --> 00:40:31.380
[APPLAUSE]

